WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
So, webR is a version of R
compiled to run in a browser.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.840
You might want to use the
containerization or sandboxing

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.170
abilities of a browser.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:11.040
You might want to run sort
of literate programming type

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.950
things where you weave software
and documentation together

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:17.280
into a single object, or
even reproducible output.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.400
You might have some data science
you want in a nice package that

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.710
is online, or you might
just be interested

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:23.950
in universal binaries.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.658
The idea of running
a piece of software

00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:27.450
on lots of different
machines, they could even

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.280
be mobile phones or tablets.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.800
These are all great
reasons that I'm not

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.050
going to talk about today.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.410
Today I'm going to talk about
why I wrote webR,

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:41.760
and it was actually for my
previous life as an academic.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.100
So when I worked for
Newcastle University,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.410
one of the things I did
was I taught R to students.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.410
And at the end of the
semester one of the things

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.080
we do is we evaluate what
the students have learned

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.170
and we do that by asking
them to write some R code

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:58.230
and we evaluate how
well that R code runs.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.780
Now during the pandemic, we
had to do that on devices

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.640
that we had no control over.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.790
People's home devices, things
where R might not even

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:06.850
be installed.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.510
We even had students doing
exams, very important exams

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.660
on a mobile phone, which is
not the best thing to do.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.690
So the question was how
do we capture that R code

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.780
and make sure they're
doing the right thing.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.910
Our first idea was
actually quite good.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:25.080
We set a server up that
certain lessons for students

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.140
R code runs it, works
out what the answer

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.700
is, and then sends it back.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.500
So this way we could
give the student

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:38.400
a website, the standard way
of evaluating and assessing

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.500
our students works.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.230
And we just plugged
into it the service

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:45.660
so it sends the code away,
remembers what the student did,

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.180
marks it and then
sends the answer back.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.650
And at first glance
it's really nice

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.150
because the students are
just writing R code

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:52.650
and they're just
seeing the results.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:53.370
They're happy.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.870
So we tested it, it worked.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.942
We put it into production.

00:01:57.942 --> 00:02:00.150
The first thing that happened
was the server crashed,

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:04.110
it fell over, and I had a room
full of very angry students.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.060
So that was not ideal.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.169
This was just before
the Christmas break.

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:11.320
So I went away over Christmas
and I thought, OK, well,

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:12.520
what can I do to solve this?

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.510
And one of the ideas I had
was to instead evaluate

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.600
that R code locally inside
the web browser instead

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:19.678
of using a server.

00:02:19.678 --> 00:02:21.220
And this makes sense
because it means

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:24.000
you're dividing the load over
lots of different machines

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.040
instead of depending
on that one machine.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.620
So the way this works is
that there's a technology

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.560
called WebAssembly that's
been around a few years now

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.420
but is getting more
popular very recently.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.810
And what it is, is
a portable binary code format.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:43.310
It's a bit like JavaScript, but
when it's in a certain format,

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:44.620
you can't read it properly.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.240
It runs in the browser.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.970
And what it does is enable
high performance applications

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:54.230
to run in a web browser
at almost native speed.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:56.960
It's supported by
most modern browsers,

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.440
and you can kind of think
of it as Java, but good.

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:05.360
The next thing that would make
this work is Emscripten,

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.480
Emscripten is a
compiler for WebAssembly,

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.970
so it converts C code
into WebAssembly.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:13.860
It's based on LLVM. Again,

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.640
it's been around for a few
years and successfully used

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:17.720
in a few projects.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.667
DOSBox and SQLite have done
it along with Spoiler Alert.

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:21.250
Sorry Winston.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.820
And also Emscripten
should work like this.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.880
You give it two commands,
there's to commands

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.430
emconfigure and emmake
you give it C code

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:34.680
and a WebAssembly
package falls out.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:35.620
When it works,

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:36.820
it's absolute magic.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:38.850
It's incredible.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:39.700
It just works.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.050
You get some C code and it runs
online. But there's a problem.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:46.480
Because R uses Fortran code.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.240
Some of the code it's
inherited had original releases

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:53.830
that were in the 70s
and even worse Emscripten

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.740
can't directly
compile Fortran code.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:00.488
Notice I say directly
because there's a trick.

00:04:00.488 --> 00:04:02.780
Unfortunately, the trick
makes things a little bit more

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:03.840
complicated.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.170
It looks like that. I'm not
going to go into detail.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:07.860
It's very messy.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:08.777
There is a link there

00:04:08.777 --> 00:04:11.330
if you're interested in
learning more, I will point out

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:12.890
I think I've got
time really quickly

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:14.557
that there is a point
in that graph that

00:04:14.557 --> 00:04:16.018
says choose your own chaos.

00:04:16.018 --> 00:04:17.810
And that's because you
choose between using

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:21.750
an ancient version of GCC or
an unreleased version of LLVM.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:24.690
So pick your poison.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:26.310
It does work.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:27.930
There's a website,
you can go to it

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.540
and you can run
R in the browser.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.980
I would say do it now, but the
conference Wi-Fi is a bit iffy

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:36.730
and it is quite a big
package to download.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:39.180
So it may not it may not work,
but when you get home, go

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:40.950
to the website, try it out.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:44.310
You can load packages I've built
just enough of the tidyverse

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:47.130
so that you can actually
load ggplot in the browser

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.540
and make a plot.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:50.900
It looks like that.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.470
And, yeah, it just works.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:55.350
Thank you very much.

